From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:55:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/h264: wait for the dependent view of the packet that
 turns it on

With frame threads, decoding of the next packet may start once the
last NAL unit that it depends on has been reached. get_last_needed_nal()
looks for that unit before the packet is decoded, and passes over the
slices of a dependent view while views_active_decode says that only the
base view is decoded.

views_active_decode is what the packets before this one left. Where a
stream brings its second view after the decoder has decoded packets
without one, the packet that brings it holds the subset SPS and the
first slices of the dependent view: h264_init_ps() decides the views
again at the first slice of the base view, in the middle of that packet
(it exports them, lets the caller choose in get_format() and sets them
up). Setup had been declared finished at that slice, the first slice of
the dependent view could not start its picture ("Too many views"), and
the second view of that access unit was not decoded. The pictures
predicted from it were then decoded from a reference that is missing,
and came out wrong without a word, until the next picture of that view
that refers to nothing before it.

A stream whose first packet has both views is not touched by this (the
context starts with no view decided, and the slices are counted). A 3D
disc can do it: its second view is read from a clip of its own, and the
first pictures of a title can reach the decoder without it.

The slices of a dependent view are now counted when this packet is one
in which the views are decided again: only the base view is decoded, and
the subset SPS that declares the views is not the one that they were
last decided with, which is the condition of h264_init_ps(). That subset
SPS is found as find_mvc_sps() finds it: the one that the PPS of the
first slice of the base view of the packet resolves to, when it declares
views, else the first one in the list that does. What the caller
chooses there cannot be known beforehand; where it chooses the base view
alone, setup is declared finished when the packet has been decoded, once
for each thread, and nothing else changes.

With one thread or slice threads nothing changes: the unit is not looked
for.

Measured with the series as of 2026-10-14, carried to FFmpeg 9.0.2, and
a stream of 75 access units made of three copies of a stream of two
views, the second view of the first 25 taken out with its subset SPS.
Without this change, with eight frame threads, the decoder says "Too
many views" once and "Missing reference picture" for four slices, gives
no second view for unit 25 and ten wrong ones after it. With it, from
unit 25 on, it gives the pictures of the decode of the whole stream, as
it does with one thread and with slice threads.
---
diff --git a/libavcodec/h264dec.c b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
--- a/libavcodec/h264dec.c
+++ b/libavcodec/h264dec.c
@@ -545,6 +545,56 @@ static av_cold void h264_decode_flush(AVCodecContext *avctx)
         FF_HW_SIMPLE_CALL(avctx, flush);
 }
 
+/**
+ * Whether the views are decided again while this packet is decoded, and a
+ * dependent view can be turned on by it. h264_init_ps() does that at the first
+ * slice of the base view when the subset SPS that declares the views is not
+ * the one that they were last decided with (the parameter sets of this packet
+ * were parsed before this is asked): it exports the views, lets the caller
+ * choose among them in get_format(), and sets them up. What the caller will
+ * choose cannot be known here.
+ *
+ * The subset SPS is found as find_mvc_sps() in h264_slice.c finds it: the one
+ * that the PPS of that slice resolves to, when it declares views, else the
+ * first one in the list that does.
+ */
+static int dependent_view_may_start(const H264Context *h)
+{
+    const SPS *sps = NULL;
+
+    if (h->views_active_decode != 1 || h->avctx->hwaccel)
+        return 0;
+
+    for (int i = 0; i < h->pkt.nb_nals; i++) {
+        const H2645NAL *nal = &h->pkt.nals[i];
+        GetBitContext gb;
+        unsigned pps_id;
+
+        if (nal->type != H264_NAL_SLICE && nal->type != H264_NAL_IDR_SLICE)
+            continue;
+        if (init_get_bits8(&gb, nal->data + 1, nal->size - 1) < 0)
+            break;
+        get_ue_golomb_long(&gb);    // first_mb_in_slice
+        get_ue_golomb_31(&gb);      // slice_type
+        pps_id = get_ue_golomb(&gb);
+        if (pps_id < MAX_PPS_COUNT)
+            sps = ff_h264_pps_sps_mvc(&h->ps, h->ps.pps_list[pps_id]);
+        break;
+    }
+    if (!sps || sps->mvc.num_views < 2) {
+        sps = NULL;
+        for (int i = 0; i < MAX_SPS_COUNT; i++) {
+            const SPS *s = h->ps.subset_sps_list[i];
+
+            if (s && s->mvc.num_views > 1) {
+                sps = s;
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    return sps && sps != h->mvc_sps;
+}
+
 static int get_last_needed_nal(H264Context *h)
 {
     int nals_needed = 0;
@@ -567,7 +617,11 @@ static int get_last_needed_nal(H264Context *h)
             nals_needed = i;
             break;
         case H264_NAL_EXTEN_SLICE:
-            if (h->views_active_decode == 1)
+            /* A dependent view that is not decoded is not waited for, unless
+             * this is the packet that may turn it on: views_active_decode is
+             * what the packets before this one left, and the first slice of
+             * the base view of this one can change it. */
+            if (h->views_active_decode == 1 && !dependent_view_may_start(h))
                 break;
             /* the MVC NAL unit header has already been consumed by the NAL parser,
              * so the slice header starts three bytes further in */
